when Spain's Supreme Court found nine  Catalan separatist leaders guilty of  sedition in the pursuit of independence  shock quickly turned to anger the  streets of Barcelona was set alight in a  series of late-night confrontations with  police and the question of Catalan  independence came to dominate yet  another election campaign Spain's fourth  in four years it's a very difficult  moment and in the end I can only speak  for myself not for others because people  have reacted in different ways Lara Maas  vidal's husband Joaquim was Catalonia's  interior minister when the region held  its illegal referendum on independence  in 2017 he was given a nine-year jail  sentence I think this ends any hope of  reconciliation and also any confidence  that people had to find a political  solution  there was a time for that but I think  it's over now the clamor for  independence that divides a country also  divides Catalans a greater proportion of  whom according to the polls don't  support the cause like business owner  Juan River Julia societies in in order  to grow to develop they need stability  stability is the main ground for  confidence when there is confidence the  companies invest consumers spend money  so everything is on hold  everything is at this moment in stable  so at this moment we are we can say that  we are society on hold it looks as if  once again a divided Catalonia will not  find comfort in the ballot box it's true  that the secessionist question is the  big issue in this election but that's  mainly because the big parties are  competing against one another over who  takes the hardest line against Catalan  independence the Catalan independence  issue has captured all this attention at  the national level because it posed like  a challenge to the status quo in the  stability of the of the state and it's a  challenge that nobody seems to have any  answer  no not so far I would say so my  impression is that this is going to be  in a sort of like a stalemate situation  for a few years the polls predict no  majority winner socialist leader Pedro  Sanchez is expected to score highest and  will likely try again to form a  coalition but no scenario offers hope to  the separatist cause in Catalonia so the  scenes that followed the sentencing of  its leaders in October may turn out to  be among the last desperate throes of a  movement with nowhere left to turn  Jonah hull al-jazeera Barcelona
 

